WEBVTT

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:07.760
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents, episode 92 for June 25th, 2026. Your daily

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.420
AI briefing. Lots of news to walk through. Let's start with what actually matters.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:20.500
GPT 5.5 Instant now handles intent and constraints more reliably while rolling out to all users

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.740
this week. Open AI released an updated GPT 5.5 Instant that improves intent understanding,

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:35.100
complex constraint handling, and recommendation quality. Open AI also announced its first custom AI

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:43.480
chip, jalapeno, built with Broadcom for chat GPT and agent workloads. Several new archive papers

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.860
introduce agent evaluation frameworks and test time adaptation techniques that address real deployment

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:57.900
gaps. The updated GPT 5.5 Instant better interprets user intent and adapts responses accordingly

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:05.060
while handling complex constraints more reliably. It also produces more useful and cohesive shopping

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:12.220
and local recommendations. The change targets open AI's most used model and is rolling out today

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:18.800
to paid users and tomorrow to free users. Builders working on recommendation or multi-constraint chat

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:25.620
flows should rerun recent prompt sets to measure reduction in clarification turns. Watch how the

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:32.220
update interacts with agent work flows that chain multiple tool calls. This development builds on the

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:39.340
frontier model's coverage from episode 90 where closed models traded capability leads and it moves

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:45.740
the open question of capability gains versus cost trajectory by tightening intent handling on the

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:53.000
model developers already used most. Open AI designed and produced its first purpose built AI chip

00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:01.020
in partnership with Broadcom for LLM workloads powering chat GPT codex the API and future agent

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:09.020
products. The move expands open AI stack from models to silicon to support larger scale. No performance

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:14.960
numbers or availability details were released. Infrastructure teams should monitor future announcements

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:21.860
on token cost impact once the chip enters production serving. This ties into the AI compute

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:28.300
an inference thread from episode 91 where custom silicon was tracked as a lever on the inference cost

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:35.360
curve. A key shoff Yael Lita is an 8 billion parameter mass diffusion language model trained

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.980
from scratch with fully bi-directional attention on 12 trillion tokens and a 25 billion token

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:52.640
instruction corpus. It improves BBH by 21.6 points, act challenge by 14.9 points, math by 14.5 points,

00:02:53.120 --> 00:03:00.680
and human avail by 16.5 points over the prior LLADA version. The model remains competitive with

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:08.280
Gwen 2.57 billion despite its non-autoraggressive training. Teams exploring diffusion based language

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.060
models should test the released weights for tasks where bi-directional context helps.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:20.380
This update narrows the open weight models gap discussed in episode 90 by showing non-autoraggressive

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:29.020
approaches closing in on standard autoregressive baselines. A new data set of 32,534 double mark

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:39.560
GCESE mock exam responses across 328 questions shows top LLMs agree with examiner consensus more

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:45.440
closely than examiners agree with each other. Agreement holds on subjective English essays and

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:51.240
messy handwritten math scripts. Model size shows little discrimination in agreement quality.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.560
Education and assessment teams can now evaluate automated marking pipelines against this frozen,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:04.520
real student distribution. Agent Odyssey procedurally generates open-ended text games with rich

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:11.100
entities and long horizon tasks to evaluate test time continual learning. The framework measures

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:17.600
game progress plus world knowledge acquisition, episodic memory, exploration, action diversity,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:24.120
and model cost. Even top agents remain far below human performance. Those stronger base models

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.420
improve results and short-term memory helps multiple paradigms. Agent developers should run their

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:36.880
systems on the generated games to quantify meaningful horizon length. This continues the agents and

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:44.600
tool use arc from episode 91, where reliability of long horizon agents was flagged as the key open

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:54.100
question. Bi-PACE replaces observation hash grouping with cosine distance clustering in the

00:04:54.100 --> 00:05:02.300
action-conditioned peer baselines for advantage estimation. On Alfworld with Quinn 2.57 billion,

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:14.100
it raises success from 90.8 to 97.1. On the 1.5 billion variant, it reaches 93.5 versus 86.7 for

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:23.080
the prior gig PO method. Overhead is 11.3% of a single training step. Agent teams using stepwise group

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:28.640
based RL should test the open implementation when credit assignment currently produces singleton

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:36.040
groups. Hybrid IR combines graph-based retrieval for structured knowledge with dense retrieval for

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:43.320
fine-grained semantics and adds an iterative retrieve reason loop. The dual path design first

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.280
pulls structured associations via graphs and then refines with dense vectors for local semantics.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.720
An iterative loop alternates retrieval and reasoning steps to recover details lost and single

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:02.000
path methods. It targets complex medical QA where single path retrieval loses either global

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:09.820
associations or local detail. Experiments on three standard medical QA benchmarks show effectiveness.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.100
Medical rag teams should compare it against their current single path setup on multi-hop clinical

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:23.960
questions. The author built a frozen 240-task EVAL set sampled from real production traffic and

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:30.740
roots every candidate model through the same prompt sequence via GPT Proto. Results show the leader

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:36.880
board winner often differs from the model that wins on the user's distribution. Gaps between first

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.540
and second place shrinked dramatically on the private set. One model that looks strong on public

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.220
benchmarks had a long tail failure mode that would have caused production incidents.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.700
Another candidate reversed its ranking entirely once the traffic distribution was applied.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:58.780
Teams should replicate the approach of freezing and versioning their own usage distribution before

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:05.520
trusting vendor reported gains. A test-time adaptation method using semi-supervised learning on

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:12.420
unlabeled inference time samples maintains robustness when supervised detectors encounter adversarial

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:20.580
humanization, new LLMs or temporal drift. The method clusters recent inference examples and generates

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:28.460
high confidence pseudo labels inside each cluster. The commercial pangram detector drops to 24.1%

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:36.820
on adversarial AI text, while the TTA approach reaches 90.5%. Code is released at the link to

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.740
The adaptation adds one forward pass per batch and runs on a sliding window of recent samples.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.780
Detection teams facing distribution shift should evaluate the semi-supervised adaptation

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:56.080
pipeline on their own traffic. Policy align synthesizes policy violating instructions from a

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:03.960
natural language safety policy and performs on policy self-distillation. An optional policy-isensitive

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:10.580
filtering step selects high impact examples for the distillation stage. It improves safety across

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:15.700
multiple models while keeping over refusal low and preserving general capabilities.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:22.620
The method also generalizes to medical, legal and financial policies without requiring new preference

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:28.860
data sets. Safety teams that receive new policies faster than they can collect preference data should

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:35.400
test the released code. Okay, let's pop the hood on test time adaptation under distribution shift.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:42.000
Everyone talks about test time adaptation as a simple, just keep learning switch. In practice,

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.620
it is a semi-supervised loop that treats incoming unlabeled samples as a mini-training set,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.980
whose homogeneity supplies the supervision signal. The method first clusters recent inference examples,

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:01.520
then uses high confidence pseudo labels within each cluster to update a lightweight adapter while

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:08.200
freezing the base detector. This adds roughly one forward pass per batch but avoids any labeled data

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:14.480
collection after deployment. The approach shines when the shift is gradual and the model family stays

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:21.040
constant. It degrades when entirely new model families appear because the homogeneity assumption breaks.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.700
When facing repeated adversarial humanization or daily drift and user writing style,

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:34.140
run the adaptation step on a sliding window of the last few hundred samples rather than the full history.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.960
So when should you actually reach for this versus the alternative?

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:44.200
Use it for gradual drift within a fixed model family and skip it when new model families arrive.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:51.640
Test the new GPT 5.5 instant on any multi-constraint prompts that previously required clarification

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:58.920
turns to measure the intent handling improvement. Run your agent on the agent-auticy game generator to

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:05.840
quantify how far its meaningful horizon extends before performance collapses. Apply the frozen 240

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:11.520
task production eVAL method from the Reddit post to the next model you are considering instead of

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.460
relying on vendor benchmarks. Try the released bypass code on your current group-based RL

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:24.200
agent setup if single-ton groups are limiting credit assignment. OpenAI's jalapeno chip is expected

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:30.040
to enter production serving for chat GPT and agent workloads in the coming months. More teams will

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:36.100
release custom eVAL sets sampled from their own traffic distributions following the pattern described

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:42.560
in the Reddit discussion. Additional papers on test time adaptation for detection and agent memory

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:49.080
are likely as distribution shift robustness becomes a standard requirement. Tomorrow keep an eye

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:56.060
on follow-up details around the jalapeno chip rollout timeline and any new test time adaptation code

00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:02.240
drops. That's models and agents for today. If you found this useful, share it with someone who's

